## Executive summary

1. The loudest instrument in an MWAA hold room during a slot-constrained departure is not the airport PA — it is a gate agent's handheld, layered over concessionaire audio and tenant televisions. MWAA specifies the shell; the airline, tenant, and handler sources sit outside the base-building spec.[^1]

2. A standard written only into the base-terminal design volume governs only the shell. The real levers are the tenant volumes, the tenant handbook, and the commissioning protocol — instruments MWAA already uses to regulate fitouts and acceptance.[^2] Airline lease exhibits are the 2039 U&L target, not a Day-1 requirement.[^1]

3. Code governs emergency communication, not the boarding call. NFPA 72 requires STI 0.45 at 90 percent of locations, averaging 0.50, yet permits an unmeasured listening test.[^3] ACRP 175 lifts the target to STI 0.50 with 10 dB(A) of signal-to-noise margin in pier hold rooms.[^4] Only a design-manual amendment makes those owner-required and mandates STIPA at commissioning; the Airports Authority Fire Marshal enforces the code floor under 49 U.S.C. §49104.[^5]

4. Redundant channels are federal law — for whichever actor owns each surface. 14 CFR Part 382 binds carriers;[^6] Section 504 and 49 CFR Part 27 bind MWAA;[^7] 28 CFR §35.160 requires "primary consideration" of the passenger's requested aid.[^8] The concessionaire's television sits in a gap only the tenant handbook closes; DOT rulemaking moves toward more accessible air travel.[^9] Chennai 2017 is the failure benchmark.[^10]

5. DCA needs a higher announcement floor than IAD, not a lower one. Sixty-seven hourly slots on a single main runway make a schedule-level miss unrecoverable.[^11] Under §9.05.8 of the 2025 Use and Lease, $1 million per beyond-perimeter pair is conditioned on a $2 DCA-vs-IAD CPE advantage; the current gap is $2.36 — a monitoring constraint, not a live threat.[^12]

6. Timing binds at Concourses C and D. Concourse E opens September 2026, making it a commissioning baseline, not a design opportunity.[^13] The July 2026 MWAA–United–USDOT announcement puts more than 5 million square feet into design at $20–22.5 billion.[^14] Acoustic ceiling treatment runs $5–15 per square foot new, $18–45 as retrofit.[^15]

7. IAD CPE rises from $12.77 to $20.71 by 2030 regardless.[^16] Against that trajectory, the stack is affordable: $12–40 million of capital — 0.13 to 0.44 percent of the signed $9.0 billion CCP, $0.03 to $0.11 per enplanement[^17] — plus roughly $600 thousand to $1.8 million a year of O&M recovered through airline rates and charges.[^18]

8. Adopt it as a Design Manual amendment executed by the VP of Design and Construction under delegated authority, with a concurrent Board information item. Name it the Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard. Never use the word "quiet" in the resolution.

