## The hold room MWAA does not own

Gate 35, DCA Terminal 2, a weekday morning. The Pelli National Hall opens into a long, hard-surfaced concourse. American, with roughly 53 percent of DCA departures,[^19] turns slot-capped narrowbodies against the 67-slot hourly cap.[^11] A gate agent calls Group 4 on a handheld, competing with a muted-but-audible television across the way, a neighboring gate's announcements in a zone the agent cannot silence, and the pier-length HVAC. A passenger with hearing aids two rows back leans in. A passenger without a smartphone watches for the visual cue that is not there. Her boarding pass says Group 3.

MWAA owns the room. It does not own the sound.

That is the operative fact. The Authority controls the shell — ceiling, finishes, base-building PA — and controls tenant fitouts through the Design Manual volumes;[^2] it does not control the airline gate microphone, the tenant television, or the handler's handheld, each governed by a separate lease or contract.[^1] Whether the executed 2025 U&L grants broadcast rights inside the leased envelope is a General Counsel diligence item; the operational reality is that gate PA is airline-run in practice.

None of this argues against the standard — it argues for writing it where the microphones live.

## What the peers actually did

Every airport the trade press calls "silent" governs exceptions. London City, silent since 2008, retains essential announcements for disruption and emergency.[^20] Helsinki-Vantaa, silent since June 2015, retains gate and emergency calls, and later added banners telling passengers to track time themselves.[^21] Schiphol runs "virtually no announcements" alongside a formal accessibility program.[^22] Copenhagen zones quiet by gate area.[^23] San Francisco, the only documented U.S. large-hub program, reports a 40 percent cut in paging announcements and 90 minutes a day removed from the International Terminal — with documented complaints about missed information.[^24] Chennai's 2017 rollout drew a backlash serious enough that officials conceded "no separate provisions for the visually-challenged are available."[^10]

No peer has published measured missed-boarding or IROPS data attributable to a silent-airport policy. An argument from peer branding is design conservatism and should be defended as such. Every one of these programs is an exception-governance regime, not an absence of PA. The variable at MWAA is not how much silence to inherit — it is what the exceptions are, who authorizes them, and in which zone.

## The floors the standard inherits — and the one it adds

The standard inherits its floors. NFPA 72 requires STI 0.45 at 90 percent of ADS locations, averaging 0.50 — but permits a listening test to satisfy it.[^3] Only the owner specification can elevate ACRP 175's targets — STI 0.50 for terminal PA, 10 dB(A) of signal-to-noise margin in pier hold rooms — to required performance and mandate occupied-condition STIPA at commissioning.[^4] The Airports Authority Fire Marshal, the AHJ inside the federal perimeter, enforces the code floor at certificate of occupancy; the owner specification enforces the delta above it.[^5]

The second floor is accessibility. 14 CFR Part 382 binds carriers on facilities they control, including captionable televisions;[^6] Section 504 and 49 CFR Part 27 bind MWAA on airport-controlled communication;[^7] 28 CFR §35.160 adds "primary consideration" of a passenger's requested aid.[^8] The concessionaire's television sits in a gap no federal regulation squarely occupies — which is why the tenant handbook is the lever, and why continuing DOT rulemaking toward accessible air travel matters even where Part 382 does not reach.[^9]

## Two airports, two calibrations

DCA closed 2025 at approximately 24.9 million passengers — down approximately 5 percent from 2024, its lowest since 2022 — absorbing the January midair collision and a fall federal government shutdown.[^25] It runs one runway capped at 67 hourly slots; the 2024 FAA Reauthorization added ten beyond-perimeter exemptions in December 2024.[^11]

A passenger who misses a boarding call is a no-show rather than a denied boarding — GAO places denied boardings at under 1 percent of actual boardings.[^26] The DCA argument is exposure. Slot scarcity makes a schedule-level miss asymmetric in a way it is not at IAD, and §9.05.8 monetizes that asymmetry at $1 million per beyond-perimeter flight pair, conditioned on a $2 CPE advantage. The current gap is $2.36, leaving $0.36 of headroom; the standard's job is to keep DCA's audible channel reliable enough that it never starts consuming it.[^12] The delivery vehicle is Terminal 2's gate-area renovations inside the $2.39 billion DCA CCP, reached through the program brief while the design is open.[^27]

IAD ran roughly 29 million passengers in 2025, 10.5 million international — both records.[^28] Its journeys are longer, its populations more multilingual, its transfers more dependent on visual and digital redundancy than on English audio. Calibrate IAD for visual and digital channels, DCA for the audible one: a common intelligibility floor with a DCA variance for announcement density and IROPS PA envelope.

## The counter-case, honestly

The strongest objection is that MWAA does not need this standard: NFPA 72, ADA Title II, and a competent commissioning specification already require most of it; a consultant-scope paragraph requiring occupied-condition STIPA would deliver most of the value without a board resolution. And the January 2025 midair is still in memory; a "quiet" framing will be read against it.

The standard concedes three things: it is not a new requirement but the integration layer across requirements that today live in separate consultants' scopes with no owner for the seam; it never carries the word "quiet" in an external MWAA document — the Schiphol and Helsinki pattern is to brand silence and govern intelligibility — so it is the Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard; and it says nothing on Day 1 about airline announcement scripting inside the leased envelope. That is a 2039 U&L review target,[^1] staged through bilateral MOUs on MWAA's data.

What it does say: what the base building must achieve, what the tenant volumes add, what each signatory MOU commits to on gate-PA discipline, and what commissioning must verify before substantial completion. Berlin Brandenburg shows what happens when that layer is missing — a life-safety and systems-integration failure against a frozen design, €2 billion closing at €7.3 billion, nine lost years.[^29] Denver's baggage system is the same class of failure: $193 million past $400 million, abandoned in 2005.[^30] Both were preventable at design.

The 17-seat Board splits three ways: a seven-seat Virginia majority, D.C. and Maryland appointees hearing from the disability communities most exposed to a reduced-information framing, and federal appointees aligned with the July 2026 Dulles announcement.[^31] A safety-and-accessibility performance floor is legible to all three. A "quiet" resolution is not. On the airline side, use-and-lease majority-in-interest carve-outs commonly exempt safety-mandated capital, which is why the standard must sit inside that carve-out from the first draft, conditioned on GC confirming the executed 2025 U&L follows the pattern.[^32]

## Where it gets written, and by when

Adopt the standard as a Design Manual amendment, executed by the VP of Design and Construction under existing delegated authority, with a concurrent information item to the Board's Business Administration Committee — a new performance section in Volume 1 and the tenant volumes, inside the FY26 revision cycle.[^2] Convert to a Board resolution only if GC's MII memo finds it materially alters airline rates and charges.

The mandatory floor at both airports: NFPA 72 STI verified by IEC 60268-16 STIPA in occupied conditions; ACRP 175 signal-to-noise margin in every pier hold room; a redundant-channel obligation — audible, visual/digital, staffed — for every safety-critical, boarding-critical, and IROPS message; PA-push authority anchored to a named position; and an emergency-notification stack on generator, UPS, and local battery with a duty-officer analog fallback for the day the managed-IT layer fails, as on 19 July 2024.[^33] Context-dependent: the DCA variance, plus SHPO-consulted preservation carve-outs for the Saarinen Main Terminal and DCA's 1941 Terminal 1.

Funding: design fees on existing consultant budgets; construction premium as incremental scope inside the $20–22.5 billion CCP envelope; mobile-notification platform as an IT acquisition under 2 CFR Part 200; lifecycle O&M of $600 thousand to $1.8 million a year through the airline rates-and-charges line.[^18]

The President and CEO owns the standard; the COO, VP D&C, and VP Airline Affairs co-sponsor. The immediate priority is the Concourse E acoustics-and-commissioning scope, which closes the baseline before other C/D design windows open. The failure mode to guard against is scope migration into an announcement-policy standard the airlines contest under the Use and Lease.

## A last image

Return to Gate 35. The ceiling and wall absorption are different; the base-building loudspeakers achieve STIPA compliance in occupied conditions. The airline gate PA is unchanged — American still calls Group 3. The concessionaire's television is captioned and muted as leases renew. The FIDS updates within a defined SLA. The passenger with hearing aids hears the call cleanly; the passenger without a smartphone reads the same information off a display. The gate agent, no longer competing with adjacent-zone spillover, spends those four minutes with the family that missed the sequence.

Nothing in that room is quiet in the marketing sense. Every message that matters lands — and that is the standard MWAA can write before the concrete cures.

